Description: Make select_del() safe after select_fini() and on double delete
 A child forked by process_fork() calls event_module.fini(), which frees
 the events table but leaves nevents untouched.  When the child later
 closes its HTTP socket, select_del() dereferences the freed (NULL)
 table and the child crashes with SIGSEGV whenever the connection is not
 the last registered event.  A second select_del() on the same event
 would also write to events[-1].
 Reset nevents in select_fini() and make select_del() a no-op for events
 that are not registered.
Author: Alexander GQ Gerasiov <gq@debian.org>
Bug-Debian: https://bugs.debian.org/989828
Bug-Debian: https://bugs.debian.org/1138586
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-08
---
diff --git a/select.c b/select.c
index e287760..70e9820 100644
--- a/select.c
+++ b/select.c
@@ -83,6 +83,7 @@ select_fini(void)
 
 	free(events);
 	events = NULL;
+	nevents = 0;
 }
 
 static int
@@ -115,6 +116,16 @@ static int
 select_del(struct event *ev, int flags)
 {
 
+	/*
+	 * Nothing to do if the event is not registered: it was already
+	 * deleted, or select_fini() has been called, which happens in a
+	 * child forked by process_fork() before it closes its sockets.
+	 */
+	if (events == NULL || ev->index < 0) {
+		ev->index = -1;
+		return (0);
+	}
+
 	assert(ev->fd < FD_SETSIZE);
 
 	switch (ev->rdwr) {
